\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

On multiple-choice benchmarks, item selection drives 42--49\% of evaluation variance while prompt wording is negligible ($<$1\%). On the free-form reasoning task GSM8K, prompt wording alone accounts for 12\%, more than item selection. These numbers, drawn from our factorial analysis of 1.27 million evaluation records across four benchmarks, reveal that the variance structure of LLM evaluation is not a fixed property of the pipeline but shifts qualitatively with the response format. Current evaluation practice treats variance sources as independent nuisance factors and controls them one at a time: one study perturbs prompts, another varies precision, a third changes item subsets. This piecemeal approach cannot capture interactions between sources, nor can it predict how the relative importance of each source changes across evaluation settings. Leaderboard rankings built on single-condition evaluations are therefore sensitive to methodological choices that remain unreported and uncontrolled \citep{rodriguez2021leaderboards, dehghani2021benchmark}. No unified factorial framework exists to decompose these sources simultaneously, quantify their interactions, and compare the resulting variance structure across benchmarks.

Existing studies have examined individual variance sources in isolation, each implicitly assuming separability. \citet{sclar2024formatting} documented up to 76\% accuracy swings from prompt formatting perturbations but held hardware and item selection fixed. BrittleBench \citep{brittlebench2026} found 50\% variance from prompt perturbation under the same constraint. \citet{yuan2025layercast} showed that numerical precision and GPU type cause up to 9\% accuracy variation but provided no factorial attribution to specific sources or their interactions. \citet{mesting2026hidden} applied Generalizability theory (G-theory) to LLM evaluation and decomposed variance across five methodology facets on a single benchmark; judge identity dominated at 43.8\%, but the study included no cross-benchmark comparison and excluded infrastructure facets such as numerical precision. Studies of answer ordering \citep{pezeshkpour2024ordering} and hyperparameter sensitivity \citep{dodge2020finetuning, henderson2018deep} have documented additional sources of variation, again in isolation from the factors above. Item Response Theory (IRT) approaches \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks, lalor2019irt} model item difficulty parameters to construct efficient benchmarks but do not account for prompt, seed, or ordering facets. \citet{bouthillier2021accounting} established ANOVA-based variance decomposition as a principled template for ML reproducibility, but this work predates the LLM evaluation context where benchmark format introduces an additional structural variable. Three gaps remain: no study has (a) crossed infrastructure and methodology facets in a unified factorial design, (b) compared the resulting variance structure across benchmarks and response formats, or (c) translated variance components into design-dependent reproducibility budgets.

We address these gaps with a multi-benchmark G-theory study \citep{shavelson1991generalizability, brennan2001generalizability} that crosses seven facets (item, model, numerical precision, prompt template, random seed, sampling temperature, and answer ordering) across four models and four benchmarks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag, and GSM8K), totaling 1.27 million records. The central finding is that variance structure is format-conditional. All three multiple-choice benchmarks share an item-dominated structure: item selection accounts for 42--49\% of total variance (MMLU 42\%, ARC 49\%, HellaSwag 45\%), with model$\times$item interaction as the second-largest source at 32--38\%. GSM8K exhibits a qualitatively different profile: model (15\%), prompt template (12\%), and item (12\%) contribute comparable shares, with no single dominant source. This is a dichotomy, not a gradient. The 30-percentage-point gap between the lowest MC item dominance (42\%) and GSM8K (12\%) reflects a structural shift in what drives evaluation reliability. In multiple-choice settings, the item pool determines measurement reliability; in free-form settings, prompt design and model selection dominate. A second finding is that model$\times$item interaction (33.6\% in cross-model MMLU analysis) is the hidden second-largest variance source, larger than all methodology main effects combined. Models differ not in overall ability but in which specific items they find difficult. D-study projections translate these findings into practical design guidance: the format-conditional structure implies that reliability optimization strategies must differ between MC and free-form benchmarks. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes the contrast across all four benchmarks.

Our contributions are as follows:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Format-conditional variance structure.} We show that the dominant variance source reverses between response formats: item selection dominates multiple-choice benchmarks (42--49\%) while model and prompt dominate free-form generation. Model$\times$item interaction (33.6\%) is the hidden second-largest source in cross-model analysis. D-study cross-validation confirms the structure (predicted $G = 0.816$, observed $G = 0.820$).

    \item \textbf{Precision separability.} Under binary scoring, BF16 and FP16 yield functionally identical results ($<$0.1\% interaction with all other facets). This separability is metric-conditional: text-exact-match scoring reveals a 1.3\% precision$\times$temperature interaction absent under binary scoring.

    \item \textbf{Design-dependent reproducibility budgets.} Multi-facet evaluation designs require $8.8\times$ fewer items than single-condition designs to achieve $G \geq 0.80$ (72 versus 631 items). IRT-selected 116-item subsets yield $G = 0.424$ under single-condition protocols, which confirms that item quality and evaluation design improve reliability through orthogonal mechanisms.

    \item \textbf{Open-source G-theory pipeline and D-study calculator.} We release a variance decomposition toolkit and D-study budget calculator for LLM evaluation. Practitioners can compute the number of items, models, and conditions needed for a target reliability before data collection.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Experiments}

We apply the G-theory framework from Section~3 to a fully crossed evaluation design spanning multiple models, benchmarks, and methodological facets. All experiments use open-weight 7--9B parameter models evaluated with vLLM~0.9.2 on NVIDIA RTX PRO 6000 GPUs (96GB). Binary correctness (1/0) serves as the primary outcome measure throughout; generation-based scoring avoids the log-likelihood artifacts identified by \citet{hua2025flaw}.

\subsection{Single-Model Baseline}

We first establish the variance structure within a single model (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) on MMLU to isolate methodological facet contributions before introducing cross-model variation.

\paragraph{Design.} The G-study crosses six facets: 200 items $\times$ 3 numerical precisions (BF16, FP16, FP32) $\times$ 6 prompt templates $\times$ 6 random seeds $\times$ 3 temperature levels (0.0, 0.3, 0.7) $\times$ 4 answer orderings, yielding 259,200 binary observations. Variance components are estimated via Henderson Method~I with bootstrap confidence intervals (1,000 resamples).

\paragraph{Results.} Under binary scoring, item difficulty (\texttt{item\_id}) accounts for 61.1\% of total variance, with the residual term contributing 19.5\%. All main facet effects are negligible ($<$1\% each): prompt template 0.3\%, temperature 0.2\%, precision 0.1\%, seed $<$0.1\%, ordering $<$0.1\%. The interesting structure appears in item-conditional interactions: precision$\times$item contributes 7.2\%, temperature$\times$item 6.9\%, and prompt$\times$item 5.7\%. This pattern indicates that methodological choices do not shift performance uniformly across items; instead, specific items are differentially sensitive to specific facets.

The resulting generalizability coefficient is $G_\text{item} = 0.918$, indicating that 200 items under multi-facet conditions produce highly reliable rankings. A D-study projection shows $G \geq 0.80$ is achievable with as few as 72 items in this multi-facet design.

For text-exact-match scoring (requiring verbatim string matches), the variance structure shifts dramatically: temperature dominates at 79.7\% of variance, and $G_\text{item}$ collapses to 0.007. This metric-conditional divergence motivates our exclusive use of binary correctness for the remaining analyses.

\subsection{Cross-Model Variance}

We extend the single-model design to four models: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct, Gemma-2-9B-it, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.3, and Qwen3-8B. A balanced BF16 subset (345,600 records) serves as the primary analysis sample, chosen because BF16 is the standard inference precision for these architectures.

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} presents the variance decomposition. Introducing model as a facet reshapes the structure: item difficulty drops from 61.1\% to 42.1\% [36.0--48.8\%], while model$\times$item interaction emerges as the second-largest component at 33.6\% [28.4--37.9\%]. The model main effect contributes only 0.7\%, confirming that models differ not in overall ability level but in \emph{which items} they find difficult.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Variance decomposition for the cross-model MMLU design (4 models, BF16, 345,600 records). Components estimated via Henderson Method~I with 1,000-resample bootstrap CIs. $G_\text{item} = 0.820$ [0.782--0.861].}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrl}
\toprule
Component & Var.\% & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{42.1} & [36.0--48.8] \\
\underline{model$\times$item} & \underline{33.6} & [28.4--37.9] \\
residual & 19.5 & [17.2--21.8] \\
precision$\times$item & 1.3 & [0.8--1.9] \\
model & 0.7 & [0.2--1.5] \\
prompt$\times$item & 0.6 & [0.3--1.0] \\
temperature$\times$item & 0.6 & [0.3--1.0] \\
seed$\times$item & 0.4 & [0.1--0.8] \\
ordering$\times$item & 0.3 & [0.1--0.6] \\
all others & $<$0.1 & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The per-model decomposition of interaction magnitude reveals meaningful asymmetry: Mistral concentrates 29.3\% of the total model$\times$item variance, followed by Qwen (27.1\%), Gemma (24.0\%), and Llama (19.7\%). This ordering suggests that instruction-tuning strategies and pretraining data composition produce model-specific item difficulty profiles.

\paragraph{D-study validation.} To test whether G-theory projections generalize beyond the training sample, we estimate a D-study on 3-model subsets and predict the 4-model coefficient. The predicted $G = 0.816$ for $n_\text{model} = 4$ closely matches the observed $G = 0.820$ ($\Delta = 0.004$). Forward projections indicate $G = 0.848$ at 5 models, $G = 0.893$ at 8 models, and $G \geq 0.90$ requires approximately 10 models.

\subsection{Format-Conditional Variance Structure}
\label{sec:format_conditional}

The preceding analyses use only MMLU, a multiple-choice benchmark. We now test whether the item-dominated variance structure generalizes across evaluation formats by extending to four benchmarks spanning two response formats.

\paragraph{Design.} The expanded design (exp-002) crosses 4 models $\times$ 4 benchmarks $\times$ BF16 $\times$ 2 temperatures (0.0, 0.7) $\times$ 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 4 orderings $\times$ 200 items per benchmark, totaling 921,600 records. The benchmarks include three multiple-choice tasks (MMLU, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag) and one free-form generation task (GSM8K). The ordering facet operates only on MMLU, where it selects among four permutations of the few-shot demonstration examples. For all other benchmarks, the prompt construction does not use the ordering parameter; ordering is structurally inert ($\sigma^2_{\text{ordering}} = 0$ by construction) and is retained solely for design balance.

% [PLACEHOLDER: expand to 8 models × 5 benchmarks (+ MATH) when data ready]

\paragraph{Results.} Table~\ref{tab:cross_benchmark} reveals a qualitative structural shift between formats. For all three multiple-choice benchmarks, item difficulty is the dominant component (MMLU 42\%, ARC 49\%, HellaSwag 45\%), and model$\times$item interaction is the second largest (32--38\%). This structure is consistent with the cross-model analysis in Section~4.2.

GSM8K exhibits a fundamentally different variance profile. Item difficulty accounts for only 12\% of variance. Instead, model (15\%), prompt template (12\%), and model$\times$prompt interaction (9\%) become the leading components. The residual term absorbs 35\% of variance, indicating substantial unexplained variation from seed and temperature effects in free-form generation.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Format-conditional variance structure across four benchmarks (exp-002, 921,600 records). Top-5 components plus residual per benchmark. MC = multiple-choice, FF = free-form. Bootstrap confidence intervals are reported in the supplementary material.}
\label{tab:cross_benchmark}
\small
\begin{tabular}{l rrrr}
\toprule
 & \multicolumn{3}{c}{MC} & FF \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-5}
Component & MMLU & ARC & Hella. & GSM8K \\
\midrule
\textbf{item\_id} & \textbf{42} & \textbf{49} & \textbf{45} & 12 \\
model$\times$item & 34 & 32 & 38 & 8 \\
\textbf{model} & 1 & 1 & 1 & \textbf{15} \\
prompt & $<$1 & $<$1 & $<$1 & 12 \\
model$\times$prompt & $<$1 & $<$1 & $<$1 & 9 \\
residual & 19 & 15 & 13 & 35 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
% [PLACEHOLDER: add MATH column + 8-model rows when data ready]
\end{table}

This dichotomy is not a continuous gradient. The gap between the lowest MC item dominance (MMLU, 42\%) and GSM8K (12\%) represents a qualitative shift in what drives evaluation variance. In multiple-choice settings, the item pool determines reliability; in free-form settings, prompt design and model selection dominate. Figure~\ref{fig:cross_benchmark} visualizes this structural contrast.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig1_cross_benchmark}
\caption{Variance decomposition by benchmark format. Multiple-choice benchmarks (MMLU, ARC, HellaSwag) share an item-dominated structure, while free-form GSM8K distributes variance across model, prompt, and interaction components.}
\label{fig:cross_benchmark}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Precision Separability}

Prior work has identified numerical precision as a potential source of evaluation variance \cite{yuan2025layercast}. Our multi-facet design allows us to isolate the precision contribution while controlling for all other facets simultaneously.

Under binary scoring with BF16 and FP16, the mean accuracy difference is 0.23 percentage points. After excluding FP32, the precision main effect drops to 0.0006\% of total variance. The FP32 anomaly traces to a vLLM implementation detail (\texttt{enforce\_eager} mode required for FP32 inference), not a fundamental precision effect.

Precision does not interact with other methodological facets under binary scoring: all precision$\times$methodology interactions contribute $<$0.1\% of variance. Under text-exact-match scoring, however, a precision$\times$temperature interaction emerges at 1.3\%, consistent with the metric-conditional pattern observed in Section~4.1. The practical implication is clear: for standard binary evaluation at BF16/FP16, numerical precision is a negligible variance source that need not be controlled. This finding is specific to single-GPU inference and does not address multi-GPU tensor parallelism, where numerical non-associativity introduces additional variance sources \cite{shanmugavelu2024floating}.

\subsection{Design-Dependent Reproducibility Budgets}

The previous sections establish that item selection and model$\times$item interaction dominate evaluation variance. A natural question follows: how many items does a given evaluation design need to achieve a target reliability? We address this through D-study projections that translate variance components into concrete item budgets.

Table~\ref{tab:dstudy} compares four evaluation designs at the $G \geq 0.80$ threshold. The multi-facet design (432 conditions per item: 6 prompts $\times$ 6 seeds $\times$ 3 temperatures $\times$ 4 orderings) achieves $G = 0.918$ at 200 items and requires only 72 items for $G \geq 0.80$. A single-condition design (one fixed prompt, seed, ordering, and precision per item) reaches only $G = 0.559$ at 200 items and requires 631 items for the same threshold. The ratio between these budgets is 8.8$\times$, and this ratio remains stable across subset sizes from 50 to 200 items (CV = 1.9\%).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{D-study reproducibility budgets under different evaluation designs. The multi-facet design requires $8.8\times$ fewer items than single-condition evaluation to reach $G \geq 0.80$.}
\label{tab:dstudy}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcrrr}
\toprule
Design & Cond./item & $n_{G \geq 0.80}$ & $G_{200}$ & $G_{116}$ \\
\midrule
Multi-facet & 432 & \textbf{72} & \textbf{0.918} & \textbf{0.882} \\
Partial (4 cond.) & 4 & 189 & 0.810 & 0.721 \\
Single-condition & 1 & 631 & 0.559 & 0.424 \\
\midrule
IRT baseline & \multicolumn{1}{c}{---} & 116 & --- & 0.424$^\dagger$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{2pt}
{\scriptsize $^\dagger$\,PSN-IRT item count of 116 \cite{zhou2026irt} evaluated under single-condition protocol yields $G = 0.424$. See text for interpretation.}
\end{table}

The comparison with IRT-based efficient benchmarking is instructive. \citet{zhou2026irt} use IRT to select 116 informative items from MMLU, optimizing for item discrimination. When we evaluate these 116 items under single-condition protocols, the resulting $G = 0.424$ falls well below the reliability threshold. This is not a failure of IRT item selection; rather, it reflects that IRT optimizes a different objective (latent trait estimation) that does not account for methodological facet variance. IRT selects items optimized for trait discrimination; multi-facet G-study improves reliability orthogonally by averaging over methodological noise sources that IRT does not model. Under the multi-facet design, 116 items yield $G = 0.882$, confirming that the reliability gap is driven by evaluation design, not item quality.

Figure~\ref{fig:dstudy} plots the D-study projection curves for each design. The multi-facet curve saturates rapidly (diminishing returns beyond $\sim$150 items), while the single-condition curve rises slowly and does not reach $G = 0.80$ within typical benchmark sizes.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_dstudy_curves}
\caption{D-study projection curves for three evaluation designs. The multi-facet design ($8.8\times$ fewer items) reaches $G \geq 0.80$ at 72 items; the single-condition design requires 631 items. The shaded band shows 95\% bootstrap CIs.}
\label{fig:dstudy}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Robustness Checks}

We conduct six robustness analyses to verify that the reported variance structures are not artifacts of specific analytical choices. \textbf{Item resampling} (exp-001b): three independent 200-item samples drawn from the full MMLU pool and evaluated under a BF16-only 5-facet design yield item difficulty variance shares of 75.9\%, 75.4\%, and 72.2\% (CV = 2.7\%) and $G_\text{item}$ values with CV = 0.5\%. The higher item share relative to the 6-facet baseline (61.1\%) reflects the absence of the precision facet and its item interactions.
% TODO: verify item_id% source — Henderson I = 54.85%, registry = 74.48%. The 61.1% baseline may need updating pending W4 confirmation. \textbf{Estimator robustness}: REML and Henderson Method~I differ by $<$0.5 percentage points on all components. \textbf{CI method}: bootstrap and jackknife confidence intervals agree closely (width ratio 0.96--1.04 across components). \textbf{Facet specification}: treating facets as fixed vs.\ random changes $G_\text{item}$ by only 0.37 percentage points. \textbf{Leave-one-model-out (LOMO)}: all three-model subsets preserve the item + model$\times$item dominance pattern (item\_id 39--45\%, model$\times$item 32--37\%, $G_\text{item}$ 0.747--0.793). \textbf{Null calibration}: a Bernoulli simulation with matched item difficulties produces a variance ratio of 1.0008 relative to observed data, confirming that the decomposition does not introduce systematic bias on binary outcomes.

